{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-36624" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3}+{y}^{2}),$ find the set of values $(x,y)$ on which $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ exists." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043502/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c154accb8c52ac5367e7632264b8bab575b5063238baa1160f65f9d1c7aa65b"} {"question" "q-36624" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, notice that $f(x,y)$ is defined only when \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}+{y}^{2} & >0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the domain of $f$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{f}={(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}>0}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivative of $f(x,y)$ with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3}+{y}^{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ is defined only for \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}+{y}^{2} & \\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the domain of ${f}_{x}$ is\n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{x}}={(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}\\ne 0}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partial derivative of $f$ with respect to $x$ exists on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{x}} & ={(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}>0}\\cap {(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}\\ne 0} \\\\  & ={(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}>0}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043502/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c154accb8c52ac5367e7632264b8bab575b5063238baa1160f65f9d1c7aa65b"} {"question" "q-36624" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043502/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8160a39dc8de687c2bddf726a8d167f584cd484dc388c7c85c8e49cacf0fbd57"} {"question" "q-36624" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043502/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c154accb8c52ac5367e7632264b8bab575b5063238baa1160f65f9d1c7aa65b"} {"question" "q-36624" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}<0}"] ["math" "{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}>0}"] ["math" "{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}\\ge 0}"] ["math" "{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}\\le 0}"] ["text" "${f}_{x}$ exists everywhere on the $xy$-plane"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043502/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c154accb8c52ac5367e7632264b8bab575b5063238baa1160f65f9d1c7aa65b"} {"question" "q-36624" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}>0}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043502/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c154accb8c52ac5367e7632264b8bab575b5063238baa1160f65f9d1c7aa65b"} {"question" "q-72003" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $f(x,y,z)=\\sqrt{xy-z},$ find the set of values $(x,y,z)$ on which $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}$ exists." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043502/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c154accb8c52ac5367e7632264b8bab575b5063238baa1160f65f9d1c7aa65b"} {"question" "q-72003" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, notice that $f(x,y,z)$ is defined only when \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}xy-z & \\ge 0 \\\\ xy & \\ge z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the domain of $f$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{f}={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xy\\ge z}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivative of $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{xy-z}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{xy-z}}\\cdot x \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{2\\sqrt{xy-z}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function ${f}_{y}(x,y,z)$ is defined only for \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}xy-z & >0 \\\\ xy & >z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the domain of ${f}_{y}$ is\n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{y}}={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xy>z}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partial derivative of $f$ with respect to $y$ exists on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{y}} & ={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xy\\ge z}\\cap {(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xy>z} \\\\  & ={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xy>z}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043502/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c154accb8c52ac5367e7632264b8bab575b5063238baa1160f65f9d1c7aa65b"} {"question" "q-72003" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043502/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8160a39dc8de687c2bddf726a8d167f584cd484dc388c7c85c8e49cacf0fbd57"} {"question" "q-72003" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043502/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c154accb8c52ac5367e7632264b8bab575b5063238baa1160f65f9d1c7aa65b"} {"question" "q-72003" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xy<z}"] ["math" "{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xy>z}"] ["math" "{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xy\\ge z}"] ["math" "{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xy\\le z}"] ["math" "{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xy\\ne z}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043502/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c154accb8c52ac5367e7632264b8bab575b5063238baa1160f65f9d1c7aa65b"} {"question" "q-72003" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xy>z}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043502/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c154accb8c52ac5367e7632264b8bab575b5063238baa1160f65f9d1c7aa65b"} {"question" "q-132645" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+z)}{y},$ find the set of values $(x,y,z)$ on which $\\frac{∂f}{∂z}$ exists." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043502/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c154accb8c52ac5367e7632264b8bab575b5063238baa1160f65f9d1c7aa65b"} {"question" "q-132645" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, notice that $f(x,y,z)$ is defined only when \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+z & >0\\,\\text{and}\\,y\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the domain of $f$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{f}={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x+z>0\\,\\,\\text{and}\\,\\,y\\ne 0}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivative of $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{z}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+z)}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x+z} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{y(x+z)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function ${f}_{z}(x,y,z)$ is defined only for \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+z & \\ne 0\\,\\text{and}\\,y\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the domain of ${f}_{z}$ is\n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{z}}={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x+z\\ne 0\\,\\,\\text{and}\\,\\,y\\ne 0}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partial derivative of $f$ with respect to $z$ exists on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{z}} & ={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x+z>0\\,\\,\\text{and}\\,\\,y\\ne 0}\\cap {(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x+z\\ne 0\\,\\,\\text{and}\\,\\,y\\ne 0} \\\\  & ={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x+z>0\\,\\,\\text{and}\\,\\,y\\ne 0}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043502/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c154accb8c52ac5367e7632264b8bab575b5063238baa1160f65f9d1c7aa65b"} {"question" "q-132645" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043502/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8160a39dc8de687c2bddf726a8d167f584cd484dc388c7c85c8e49cacf0fbd57"} {"question" "q-132645" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043502/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c154accb8c52ac5367e7632264b8bab575b5063238baa1160f65f9d1c7aa65b"} {"question" "q-132645" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x+z>0\\,\\,\\text{and}\\,\\,y>0}"] ["math" "{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x+z>0\\,\\,\\text{and}\\,\\,y\\ne 0}"] ["math" "{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x+z>0}"] ["math" "{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x+z\\ne 0\\,\\,\\text{and}\\,\\,y>0}"] ["math" "{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x+z\\ne 0}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043502/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c154accb8c52ac5367e7632264b8bab575b5063238baa1160f65f9d1c7aa65b"} {"question" "q-132645" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x+z>0\\,\\,\\text{and}\\,\\,y\\ne 0}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043502/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c154accb8c52ac5367e7632264b8bab575b5063238baa1160f65f9d1c7aa65b"} {"question" "q-72000" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y+z),$ find the set of values $(x,y,z)$ on which $\\frac{∂f}{∂z}$ exists." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043502/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c154accb8c52ac5367e7632264b8bab575b5063238baa1160f65f9d1c7aa65b"} {"question" "q-72000" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, notice that $f(x,y,z)$ is defined only when \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y+z & >0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the domain of $f$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{f}={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x+y+z>0}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivative of $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{z}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y+z)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x+y+z}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function ${f}_{z}(x,y,z)$ is defined only for \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y+z & \\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the domain of ${f}_{z}$ is\n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{z}}={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x+y+z\\ne 0}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partial derivative of $f$ with respect to $z$ exists on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{z}} & ={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x+y+z>0}\\cap {(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x+y+z\\ne 0} \\\\  & ={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x+y+z>0}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043502/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c154accb8c52ac5367e7632264b8bab575b5063238baa1160f65f9d1c7aa65b"} {"question" "q-72000" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043502/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8160a39dc8de687c2bddf726a8d167f584cd484dc388c7c85c8e49cacf0fbd57"} {"question" "q-72000" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043502/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c154accb8c52ac5367e7632264b8bab575b5063238baa1160f65f9d1c7aa65b"} {"question" "q-72000" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x+y+z=0}"] ["math" "{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x+y+z>0}"] ["math" "{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x+y+z\\ge 0}"] ["math" "{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x+y+z\\le 0}"] ["math" "{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x+y+z\\ne 0}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043502/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c154accb8c52ac5367e7632264b8bab575b5063238baa1160f65f9d1c7aa65b"} {"question" "q-72000" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x+y+z>0}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043502/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c154accb8c52ac5367e7632264b8bab575b5063238baa1160f65f9d1c7aa65b"} {"question" "q-132643" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{z}{\\sqrt{x-y}},$ find the set of values $(x,y,z)$ on which $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}$ exists." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043502/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c154accb8c52ac5367e7632264b8bab575b5063238baa1160f65f9d1c7aa65b"} {"question" "q-132643" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, notice that $f(x,y,z)$ is defined only when \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-y & >0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the domain of $f$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{f}={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x-y>0}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivative of $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{z}{\\sqrt{x-y}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{z}{2\\sqrt{(x-y)^{3}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function ${f}_{y}(x,y,z)$ is defined only for \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-y>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the domain of ${f}_{y}$ is\n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{y}}={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x-y>0}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partial derivative of $f$ with respect to $y$ exists on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{y}} & ={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x-y>0}\\cap {(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x-y>0} \\\\  & ={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x-y>0}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043502/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c154accb8c52ac5367e7632264b8bab575b5063238baa1160f65f9d1c7aa65b"} {"question" "q-132643" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043502/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8160a39dc8de687c2bddf726a8d167f584cd484dc388c7c85c8e49cacf0fbd57"} {"question" "q-132643" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043502/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c154accb8c52ac5367e7632264b8bab575b5063238baa1160f65f9d1c7aa65b"} {"question" "q-132643" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x-y>0}"] ["math" "{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x-y\\ne 0}"] ["math" "{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x-z>0\\,\\text{and}\\,z\\ne 0}"] ["math" "{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x-z\\ne 0\\,\\text{and}\\,z>0}"] ["math" "{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,z>0}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043502/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c154accb8c52ac5367e7632264b8bab575b5063238baa1160f65f9d1c7aa65b"} {"question" "q-132643" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x-y>0}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043502/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c154accb8c52ac5367e7632264b8bab575b5063238baa1160f65f9d1c7aa65b"} {"question" "q-72005" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $g(x,y)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂g}{∂x}=\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂g}{∂y}=\\frac{2xy}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}},\n$$\n\nfind the set of values $(x,y)$ where all partial derivatives of $g$ exist." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043502/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c154accb8c52ac5367e7632264b8bab575b5063238baa1160f65f9d1c7aa65b"} {"question" "q-72005" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, notice that $g(x,y)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})$ is defined only when ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>0.$ So, the domain of $g$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{g}={(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>0}.\n$$\n\nAs for the partial derivatives, we have the following.\n\n- The function ${g}_{x}(x,y)=\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})$ is defined only when ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>0.$ So, the domain of ${g}_{x}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{g}_{x}}={(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>0}.\n$$\n\n- The function ${g}_{y}(x,y)=\\frac{2xy}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ is defined only when ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of ${g}_{y}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{g}_{y}}={(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ne 0}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partials of $g$ exist on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{g}\\cap {D}_{{g}_{x}}\\cap {D}_{{g}_{y}} & ={(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>0}\\cap {(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>0}\\cap {(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ne 0} \\\\  & ={(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>0}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043502/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c154accb8c52ac5367e7632264b8bab575b5063238baa1160f65f9d1c7aa65b"} {"question" "q-72005" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043502/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8160a39dc8de687c2bddf726a8d167f584cd484dc388c7c85c8e49cacf0fbd57"} {"question" "q-72005" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043502/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c154accb8c52ac5367e7632264b8bab575b5063238baa1160f65f9d1c7aa65b"} {"question" "q-72005" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<0}"] ["math" "{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=0}"] ["math" "{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>0}"] ["math" "{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ge 0}"] ["math" "{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ne 0}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043502/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c154accb8c52ac5367e7632264b8bab575b5063238baa1160f65f9d1c7aa65b"} {"question" "q-72005" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>0}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043502/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c154accb8c52ac5367e7632264b8bab575b5063238baa1160f65f9d1c7aa65b"} {"question" "q-132651" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $f(x,y)=\\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{y}$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{1}{2y\\sqrt{x}},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=-\\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{{y}^{2}},\n$$\n\nfind the set of values $(x,y)$ where all partial derivatives of $f$ exist." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043502/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c154accb8c52ac5367e7632264b8bab575b5063238baa1160f65f9d1c7aa65b"} {"question" "q-132651" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, notice that $f(x,y)=\\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{y}$ is defined only when $x\\ge 0$ and $y\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of $f$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{f}={(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\ge 0,y\\ne 0}.\n$$\n\nAs for the partial derivatives, we have the following.\n\n- The function ${f}_{x}(x,y)=\\frac{1}{2y\\sqrt{x}}$ is defined only when $x>0$ and $y\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of ${f}_{x}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{x}}={(x,y)\\,:\\,x>0,y\\ne 0}.\n$$\n\n- The function ${f}_{y}(x,y)=-\\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{{y}^{2}}$ is defined only when $x\\ge 0$ and $y\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of ${f}_{y}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{y}}={(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\ge 0,y\\ne 0}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partials of $f$ exist on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{x}}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{y}} & ={(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\ge 0,y\\ne 0}\\cap {(x,y)\\,:\\,x>0,y\\ne 0}\\cap {(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\ge 0,y\\ne 0} \\\\  & ={(x,y)\\,:\\,x>0,y\\ne 0}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043502/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c154accb8c52ac5367e7632264b8bab575b5063238baa1160f65f9d1c7aa65b"} {"question" "q-132651" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043502/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8160a39dc8de687c2bddf726a8d167f584cd484dc388c7c85c8e49cacf0fbd57"} {"question" "q-132651" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043502/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c154accb8c52ac5367e7632264b8bab575b5063238baa1160f65f9d1c7aa65b"} {"question" "q-132651" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{(x,y)\\,:\\,x<0,\\,y\\ne 0}"] ["math" "{(x,y)\\,:\\,x>0,\\,y<0}"] ["math" "{(x,y)\\,:\\,x>0,\\,y>0}"] ["math" "{(x,y)\\,:\\,x>0,\\,y\\ne 0}"] ["math" "{(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\ge 0,\\,y\\ne 0}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043502/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c154accb8c52ac5367e7632264b8bab575b5063238baa1160f65f9d1c7aa65b"} {"question" "q-132651" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{(x,y)\\,:\\,x>0,\\,y\\ne 0}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043502/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c154accb8c52ac5367e7632264b8bab575b5063238baa1160f65f9d1c7aa65b"} {"question" "q-132650" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $g(x,y,z)=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1)}{z}$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂g}{∂x}=\\frac{2x}{z({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1)},\\,\\frac{∂g}{∂y}=\\frac{2y}{z({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1)},\\,\\frac{∂g}{∂z}=-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1)}{{z}^{2}},\n$$\n\nfind the set of values $(x,y,z)$ where all partial derivatives of $g$ exist." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043502/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c154accb8c52ac5367e7632264b8bab575b5063238baa1160f65f9d1c7aa65b"} {"question" "q-132650" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, notice that $g(x,y,z)=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1)}{z}$ is defined only when ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1>0$ and $z\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of $g$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{g}={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>1,\\,z\\ne 0}.\n$$\n\nAs for the partial derivatives, we have the following.\n\n- The function ${g}_{x}(x,y,z)=\\frac{2x}{z({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1)}$ is defined only when ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1\\ne 0$ and $z\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of ${g}_{x}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{g}_{x}}={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ne 1,\\,z\\ne 0}.\n$$\n\n- Similarly, the domain of ${g}_{y}(x,y,z)$ is the same: \n\n$$\n{D}_{{g}_{y}}={D}_{{g}_{x}}={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ne 1,\\,z\\ne 0}.\n$$\n\n- The function ${g}_{z}(x,y,z)=-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1)}{{z}^{2}}$ is defined only when ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1>0$ and $z\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of ${g}_{z}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{g}_{z}}={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>1,\\,z\\ne 0}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partials of $g$ exist on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{g}\\cap {D}_{{g}_{x}}\\cap {D}_{{g}_{y}}\\cap {D}_{{g}_{z}} & ={D}_{g}\\cap {D}_{{g}_{x}}\\cap {D}_{{g}_{z}} \\\\  & ={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>1,\\,z\\ne 0} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\cap {(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ne 1,\\,z\\ne 0} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\cap {(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>1,\\,z\\ne 0} \\\\  & ={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>1,\\,z\\ne 0}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043502/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c154accb8c52ac5367e7632264b8bab575b5063238baa1160f65f9d1c7aa65b"} {"question" "q-132650" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043502/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8160a39dc8de687c2bddf726a8d167f584cd484dc388c7c85c8e49cacf0fbd57"} {"question" "q-132650" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043502/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c154accb8c52ac5367e7632264b8bab575b5063238baa1160f65f9d1c7aa65b"} {"question" "q-132650" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>0,\\,z>0}"] ["math" "{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>1,\\,z\\ne 0}"] ["math" "{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>1}"] ["math" "{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ne 1,\\,z\\ne 0}"] ["math" "{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ne 1}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043502/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c154accb8c52ac5367e7632264b8bab575b5063238baa1160f65f9d1c7aa65b"} {"question" "q-132650" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>1,\\,z\\ne 0}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043502/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c154accb8c52ac5367e7632264b8bab575b5063238baa1160f65f9d1c7aa65b"} {"question" "q-72025" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x+y+z)$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=\\frac{1}{1+(x+y+z)^{2}},\n$$\n\nfind the set of values $(x,y,z)$ on which all partial derivatives of $f$ exist." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043502/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c154accb8c52ac5367e7632264b8bab575b5063238baa1160f65f9d1c7aa65b"} {"question" "q-72025" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, notice that $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x+y+z)$ is defined for any $x$, $y$, and $z.$ So, the domain of $f$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{f}={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )}.\n$$\n\nAs for the partial derivatives, we have the following.\n\n- The function ${f}_{x}=\\frac{1}{1+(x+y+z)^{2}}$ is defined for any $x$, $y$, and $z.$ So, the domain of ${f}_{x}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{x}}={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )}.\n$$\n\n- Similarly, the domains of ${f}_{y}$ and ${f}_{z}$ are\n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{y}}={D}_{{f}_{z}}={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the partial derivatives of $f$ exist on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{x}}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{y}}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{z}} & ={D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{x}} \\\\  & ={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )}\\cap {(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )} \\\\  & ={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043502/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c154accb8c52ac5367e7632264b8bab575b5063238baa1160f65f9d1c7aa65b"} {"question" "q-72025" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043502/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8160a39dc8de687c2bddf726a8d167f584cd484dc388c7c85c8e49cacf0fbd57"} {"question" "q-72025" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043502/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c154accb8c52ac5367e7632264b8bab575b5063238baa1160f65f9d1c7aa65b"} {"question" "q-72025" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x+y+z)^{2}>1}"] ["math" "{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x+y+z>-1}"] ["math" "{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x+y+z\\ne 0}"] ["math" "{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x+y+z\\ne 1}"] ["math" "{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043502/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c154accb8c52ac5367e7632264b8bab575b5063238baa1160f65f9d1c7aa65b"} {"question" "q-72025" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043502/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c154accb8c52ac5367e7632264b8bab575b5063238baa1160f65f9d1c7aa65b"} {"question" "q-72008" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $g(x,y)=\\sqrt{x}\\,{e}^{x/y}$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂g}{∂x}=\\frac{(2x+y){e}^{x/y}}{2y\\sqrt{x}},\\,\\frac{∂g}{∂y}=-\\frac{\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}\\,{e}^{x/y}}{{y}^{2}},\n$$\n\nfind the set of values $(x,y)$ where all partial derivatives of $g$ exist." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043502/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c154accb8c52ac5367e7632264b8bab575b5063238baa1160f65f9d1c7aa65b"} {"question" "q-72008" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, notice that $g(x,y)=\\sqrt{x}\\,{e}^{x/y}$ is defined only when $x\\ge 0$ and $y\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of $g$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{g}={(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\ge 0,y\\ne 0}.\n$$\n\nAs for the partial derivatives, we have the following.\n\n- The function ${g}_{x}(x,y)=\\frac{(2x+y){e}^{x/y}}{2y\\sqrt{x}}$ is defined only when $x>0$ and $y\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of ${g}_{x}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{g}_{x}}={(x,y)\\,:\\,x>0,y\\ne 0}.\n$$\n\n- The function ${g}_{y}(x,y)=-\\frac{\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}{e}^{x/y}}{{y}^{2}}$ is defined only when $x\\ge 0$ and $y\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of ${g}_{x}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{g}_{y}}={(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\ge 0,y\\ne 0}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partials of $g$ exist on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{g}\\cap {D}_{{g}_{x}}\\cap {D}_{{g}_{y}} & ={(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\ge 0,y\\ne 0}\\cap {(x,y)\\,:\\,x>0,y\\ne 0}\\cap {(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\ge 0,y\\ne 0} \\\\  & ={(x,y)\\,:\\,x>0,y\\ne 0}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043502/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c154accb8c52ac5367e7632264b8bab575b5063238baa1160f65f9d1c7aa65b"} {"question" "q-72008" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043502/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8160a39dc8de687c2bddf726a8d167f584cd484dc388c7c85c8e49cacf0fbd57"} {"question" "q-72008" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043502/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c154accb8c52ac5367e7632264b8bab575b5063238baa1160f65f9d1c7aa65b"} {"question" "q-72008" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{(x,y)\\,:\\,x=0,y\\ne 0}"] ["math" "{(x,y)\\,:\\,x>0,y>0}"] ["math" "{(x,y)\\,:\\,x>0,y\\ne 0}"] ["math" "{(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\ge 0,y>0}"] ["math" "{(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\ge 0,y\\ne 0}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043502/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c154accb8c52ac5367e7632264b8bab575b5063238baa1160f65f9d1c7aa65b"} {"question" "q-72008" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{(x,y)\\,:\\,x>0,y\\ne 0}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043502/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c154accb8c52ac5367e7632264b8bab575b5063238baa1160f65f9d1c7aa65b"} {"question" "q-36504" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following statements are true about the function $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{x}{x+y})?$\n\n- Both partial derivatives exist at the point $(1,1)$\n- ${f}_{x}(0,1)$ is defined\n- ${f}_{y}(0,1)$ is defined" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043502/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c154accb8c52ac5367e7632264b8bab575b5063238baa1160f65f9d1c7aa65b"} {"question" "q-36504" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we note that $f$ is defined only when\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{x+y}>0.\n$$\n\nAt points in the domain of $f,$ we can compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{x}{x+y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{x+y}{x}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{x}{x+y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{x+y}{x}\\cdot \\frac{1\\cdot (x+y)-x\\cdot 1}{(x+y)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x+y}{x}\\cdot \\frac{y}{(x+y)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{x(x+y)} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{x}{x+y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{x+y}{x}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{x}{x+y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{x+y}{x}\\cdot (-\\frac{x}{(x+y)^{2}}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{x+y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. The function is defined at $(1,1),$ since\n\n$$\nf(1,1)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{1+1})=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}),\n$$\n\nand both partial derivatives are defined there:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(1,1) & =\\frac{1}{1(1+1)}=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {f}_{y}(1,1) & =-\\frac{1}{1+1}=-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. At $(0,1),$\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{x+y}=\\frac{0}{0+1}=0,\n$$\n\nso\n\n$$\nf(0,1)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0),\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined. Therefore, ${f}_{x}(0,1)$ is not defined.\n\n- Statement III is false for the same reason. Since $f$ is not defined at $(0,1),$ the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(0,1)$ is not defined. The simplified expression $-\\frac{1}{x+y}$ cannot be used to define the partial derivative at a point outside the domain of $f.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043502/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c154accb8c52ac5367e7632264b8bab575b5063238baa1160f65f9d1c7aa65b"} {"question" "q-36504" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043502/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8160a39dc8de687c2bddf726a8d167f584cd484dc388c7c85c8e49cacf0fbd57"} {"question" "q-36504" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043502/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c154accb8c52ac5367e7632264b8bab575b5063238baa1160f65f9d1c7aa65b"} {"question" "q-36504" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043502/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c154accb8c52ac5367e7632264b8bab575b5063238baa1160f65f9d1c7aa65b"} {"question" "q-36504" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043502/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c154accb8c52ac5367e7632264b8bab575b5063238baa1160f65f9d1c7aa65b"} {"question" "q-36522" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following statements are true about the function $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{{z}^{2}}{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}?$\n\n- The partial derivative with respect to $x$ is defined at $(1,-1,0)$\n- ${f}_{x}(0,1,1)$ is defined\n- ${f}_{z}(1,1,0)$ is defined" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043502/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c154accb8c52ac5367e7632264b8bab575b5063238baa1160f65f9d1c7aa65b"} {"question" "q-36522" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we compute the partial derivatives of $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})^{2}}\\cdot (2x) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2x{z}^{2}}{({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})^{2}}\\cdot (-2y) \\\\  & =\\frac{2y{z}^{2}}{({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =\\frac{2z}{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine the statements one-by-one.\n\n- Statement I is false. If we substitute $(1,-1,0)$ into $f$, we get\n\n$$\nf(1,-1,0)=\\frac{(0)^{2}}{(1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}}=\\frac{0}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined since we have division by zero. Since the function is not defined at the point, the derivative is also undefined.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, the function $f$ is defined at $(0,1,1)$, where we have \n\n$$\nf(0,1,1)=\\frac{(1)^{2}}{(0)^{2}-(1)^{2}}=-1,\n$$\n\nand ${f}_{x}$ is also defined at $(0,1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(0,1,1) & =-\\frac{2(0)(1)}{(0)^{2}-(1)^{2}}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. If we substitute $(1,1,0)$ into $f$, we get\n\n$$\nf(1,1,0)=\\frac{(0)^{2}}{(1)^{2}-(1)^{2}}=\\frac{0}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined since we have division by zero. Since the function is not defined at the point, the derivative is also undefined.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'II only'." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043502/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c154accb8c52ac5367e7632264b8bab575b5063238baa1160f65f9d1c7aa65b"} {"question" "q-36522" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043502/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8160a39dc8de687c2bddf726a8d167f584cd484dc388c7c85c8e49cacf0fbd57"} {"question" "q-36522" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043502/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c154accb8c52ac5367e7632264b8bab575b5063238baa1160f65f9d1c7aa65b"} {"question" "q-36522" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043502/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c154accb8c52ac5367e7632264b8bab575b5063238baa1160f65f9d1c7aa65b"} {"question" "q-36522" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043502/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c154accb8c52ac5367e7632264b8bab575b5063238baa1160f65f9d1c7aa65b"} {"question" "q-72022" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following statements are true about the function $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy(z-1))?$\n\n- The partial derivative with respect to $z$ is defined at $(3,2,1)$\n- ${f}_{y}(1,1,2)$ is defined\n- ${f}_{x}(1,2,3)$ is defined" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043502/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c154accb8c52ac5367e7632264b8bab575b5063238baa1160f65f9d1c7aa65b"} {"question" "q-72022" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we compute the partial derivatives of $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{1}{xy(z-1)}\\cdot y(z-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{1}{xy(z-1)}\\cdot x(z-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{y} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =\\frac{1}{xy(z-1)}\\cdot xy \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{z-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine the statements one-by-one.  \n\n- Statement I is false. If we substitute $(3,2,1)$ into $f$, we get\n\n$$\nf(3,2,1)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3\\cdot 2\\cdot (1-1))=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0),\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined.\n\n- Statement II is true. If we substitute $(1,1,2)$ into $f$, we get\n\n$$\nf(1,1,2)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1\\cdot 1\\cdot (2-1))=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)=0,\n$$\n\nand ${f}_{y}$ is also defined at $(1,1,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(1,1,2) & =\\frac{1}{1}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. If we substitute $(1,2,3)$ into $f$, we get\n\n$$\nf(1,2,3)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1\\cdot 2\\cdot (3-1))=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4),\n$$\n\nand ${f}_{x}$ is also defined at $(1,2,3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(1,2,3) & =\\frac{1}{1}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'II and III  only'." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043502/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c154accb8c52ac5367e7632264b8bab575b5063238baa1160f65f9d1c7aa65b"} {"question" "q-72022" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043502/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8160a39dc8de687c2bddf726a8d167f584cd484dc388c7c85c8e49cacf0fbd57"} {"question" "q-72022" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043502/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c154accb8c52ac5367e7632264b8bab575b5063238baa1160f65f9d1c7aa65b"} {"question" "q-72022" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043502/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c154accb8c52ac5367e7632264b8bab575b5063238baa1160f65f9d1c7aa65b"} {"question" "q-72022" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II and III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043502/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c154accb8c52ac5367e7632264b8bab575b5063238baa1160f65f9d1c7aa65b"} {"question" "q-132675" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following statements are true about the function $f(x,y,z)=z\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)?$\n\n- The partial derivative with respect to $y$ is defined at $(-1,-1,0)$\n- ${f}_{x}(0,1,1)$ is defined\n- ${f}_{z}(1,-1,1)$ is defined" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043502/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c154accb8c52ac5367e7632264b8bab575b5063238baa1160f65f9d1c7aa65b"} {"question" "q-132675" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we compute the partial derivatives of $f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}z\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy) \\\\  & =z\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)) \\\\  & =\\frac{z}{x} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}z\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy) \\\\  & =z\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)) \\\\  & =\\frac{z}{y} \\\\ {f}_{z}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}z\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂z}(z) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine the statements one-by-one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the function $f$ is defined at $(-1,-1,0)$, where we have \n\n$$\nf(-1,-1,0)=0\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡((-1)\\cdot (-1))=0,\n$$\n\nand ${f}_{y}$ is also defined at $(-1,-1,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(-1,-1,0) & =\\frac{0}{(-1)}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. If we substitute $(0,1,1)$ into ${f}_{x}$, we get\n\n$$\n{f}_{x}(0,1,1)=\\frac{1}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined since we have division by zero.\n\n- Statement III is also false. If we substitute $(1,-1,1)$ into ${f}_{z}$, we get\n\n$$\n{f}_{z}(1,-1,1)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1\\cdot (-1))=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(-1),\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined since $\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is defined only for $x>0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043502/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c154accb8c52ac5367e7632264b8bab575b5063238baa1160f65f9d1c7aa65b"} {"question" "q-132675" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043502/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8160a39dc8de687c2bddf726a8d167f584cd484dc388c7c85c8e49cacf0fbd57"} {"question" "q-132675" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043502/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c154accb8c52ac5367e7632264b8bab575b5063238baa1160f65f9d1c7aa65b"} {"question" "q-132675" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043502/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c154accb8c52ac5367e7632264b8bab575b5063238baa1160f65f9d1c7aa65b"} {"question" "q-132675" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043502/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c154accb8c52ac5367e7632264b8bab575b5063238baa1160f65f9d1c7aa65b"} {"question" "q-132728" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following statements are true about the function $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2)?$\n\n- Both partial derivatives exist at the point $(0,2)$\n- ${f}_{x}(1,0)$ is defined\n- ${f}_{y}(1,1)$ is defined" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043502/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c154accb8c52ac5367e7632264b8bab575b5063238baa1160f65f9d1c7aa65b"} {"question" "q-132728" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we compute the partial derivatives of $f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2) \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2) \\\\  & =\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine the statements one-by-one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the function $f$ is defined at $(0,2)$, where we have \n\n$$\nf(0,2)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({0}^{2}+{2}^{2}-2)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,\n$$\n\nand both partial derivatives are also defined at $(0,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(0,2) & =\\frac{2(0)}{{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}-2}=0 \\\\ {f}_{y}(0,2) & =\\frac{2(2)}{{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}-2}=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. If we substitute $(1,0)$ into $f$, we get\n\n$$\nf(1,0)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({1}^{2}+{0}^{2}-2)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(-1)\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined since $\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is defined only for $x>0.$ Since the function is not defined at the point, the derivative is also undefined.\n\n- Statement III is also false. If we substitute $(1,1)$ into $f$, we get\n\n$$\nf(1,0)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({1}^{2}+{1}^{2}-2)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0)\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined since $\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is defined only for $x>0.$ Since the function is not defined at the point, the derivative is also undefined.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043502/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c154accb8c52ac5367e7632264b8bab575b5063238baa1160f65f9d1c7aa65b"} {"question" "q-132728" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043502/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8160a39dc8de687c2bddf726a8d167f584cd484dc388c7c85c8e49cacf0fbd57"} {"question" "q-132728" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043502/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c154accb8c52ac5367e7632264b8bab575b5063238baa1160f65f9d1c7aa65b"} {"question" "q-132728" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043502/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c154accb8c52ac5367e7632264b8bab575b5063238baa1160f65f9d1c7aa65b"} {"question" "q-132728" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043502/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c154accb8c52ac5367e7632264b8bab575b5063238baa1160f65f9d1c7aa65b"} {"question" "e-3449" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $f(x,y)=\\dfrac yx,$ find the set of values $(x,y)$ on which $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}$ exists." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043502/example-3449.json" "file_sha256" "83fefe7d6f9f0d3577a4fe4106fa1bf24644fe884bfda1bdd8d5d545c4a2cb01"} {"question" "e-3449" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Remember that the partial derivative of a function exists where the original function is defined *and* the partial derivative is defined. \n\nFirst, notice that $f(x,y)$ is not defined when $x=0.$ So, the domain of $f$ is \n\n$$\nD_f = \\{(x,y) \\, : \\, x\\ne 0 \\}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivative of $f(x,y)$ with respect to $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{y}{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe partial derivative $f_y(x,y)$ is also not defined for $x=0.$ So, the domain of $f_y$ is\n\n$$\nD_{f_y} = \\{(x,y) \\, : \\, x\\ne 0 \\}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the set where  $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}$ exists, we take the intersection of the domain of the original function $(D_f)$ with the domain of the partial derivative $(D_{f_y})$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{y}} & ={(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\ne 0}\\cap {(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\ne 0} \\\\  & ={(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\ne 0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}$ exists on the set $\\{ (x,y)\\, : \\, x \\neq 0 \\}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043502/example-3449.json" "file_sha256" "83fefe7d6f9f0d3577a4fe4106fa1bf24644fe884bfda1bdd8d5d545c4a2cb01"} {"question" "e-6109" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $f(x,y)= \\ln\\left(\\dfrac{x}{y^2}\\right)$ and\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} = \\dfrac{1}{x}, \\qquad\n\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y} = -\\dfrac{2}{y},\n$$\n\nfind the set of values $(x,y)$ on which all partial derivatives of $f$ exist." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043502/example-6109.json" "file_sha256" "12dade5964e09c660caf0163824bf4f4880eb68c5e5677af675e33b62fc1776a"} {"question" "e-6109" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Remember that the partial derivatives of a function exist where the original function is defined and the partial derivatives are defined.\n\nFirst, notice that $f(x,y) = \\ln\\left(\\dfrac{x}{y^2}\\right)$ is defined only when $\\dfrac{x}{y^2}>0.$ So, the domain of $f$ is \n\n$$\nD_f = \\{(x,y) \\, : \\, x > 0, \\: y \\neq 0 \\}.\n$$\n\nAs for the partial derivatives, we have the following:\n\n- The partial derivative $f_x(x,y) = \\dfrac{1}{x}$ is defined only when $x \\neq 0.$ So, the domain of $f_x$ is \n\n$$\nD_{f_x} = \\{(x,y) \\, : \\, x \\neq 0 \\}.\n$$\n\n- The partial derivative $f_y(x,y) =  -\\dfrac{2}{y}$ is defined only when $y \\neq 0.$ So, the domain of $f_y$ is \n\n$$\nD_{f_y} = \\{(x,y) \\, : \\, y \\neq 0 \\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partial derivatives of $f$ exist on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{x}}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{y}} & ={(x,y)\\,:\\,x>0,\\,y\\ne 0}\\cap {(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\ne 0}\\cap {(x,y)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0} \\\\  & ={(x,y)\\,:\\,x>0,\\,y\\ne 0}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043502/example-6109.json" "file_sha256" "12dade5964e09c660caf0163824bf4f4880eb68c5e5677af675e33b62fc1776a"} {"question" "e-3450" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $f(x,y,z)=xz\\ln{(x+y+z)}?$\n\n- $f_x(-1,-1,-1)$ is defined\n\n- $f_y(1,0,-1)$ is defined\n\n- $f_z(1,1,1)$ is defined" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043502/example-3450.json" "file_sha256" "b88c234446a6e1571df86d96d558b445139e992d5eaba94d472e593bd3fedf1e"} {"question" "e-3450" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we compute the partial derivatives of $f(x,y,z)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =z\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y+z)+\\frac{xz}{x+y+z} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{xz}{x+y+z} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y+z)+\\frac{xz}{x+y+z}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine the statement one-by-one.\n\n- Statement I is false. If we substitute $(-1,-1,-1)$ into $f,$ we get\n\n$$\nf(-1,-1,-1) = (-1)(-1)\\ln{(-1-1-1)} = \\ln{(-3)},\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined since the logarithm exists only for positive arguments. Therefore, $f_x$ cannot exist at this point.\n\n- Statement II is false. If we substitute $(1,0,-1)$ into $f_y,$ we get\n\n$$\nf(1,0,-1) = \\dfrac{(1)(-1)}{1+0-1} = \\dfrac{-1}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined since we have division by zero. Since the function is not defined at the point, the derivative is also undefined.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, the function $f$ is defined at $(1,1,1)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nf(1,1,1)= (1)(1)\\ln(1+1+1)=\\ln(3)\n$$\n\nLikewise $f_z$ is also defined at $(1,1,1)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{z}(1,1,1) & =(1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+1+1)+\\frac{(1)(1)}{1+1+1}=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)+\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043502/example-3450.json" "file_sha256" "b88c234446a6e1571df86d96d558b445139e992d5eaba94d472e593bd3fedf1e"}] "usage" {"q-36522" [] "q-36504" [] "e-6109" [] "e-3449" [] "e-3450" []} "basis" 1624 "retractions" [[17592186124911 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true about the function $f(x, y) = \\ln (\\frac{x}{x + y})$?\n\n1. Both partial derivatives exist at the point $(1, 1)$\n2. $f_{x}(0, 1)$ is defined\n3. $f_{y}(0, 1)$ is defined"] [17592186124916 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true about the function $f(x, y, z) = \\frac{z^{2}}{x^{2} - y^{2}}$?\n\n1. The partial derivative with respect to $x$ is defined at $(1,-1, 0)$\n2. $f_{x}(0, 1, 1)$ is defined\n3. $f_{z}(1, 1, 0)$ is defined"] [17592186124184 :question/problem "Given that $f(x,y)=\\dfrac yx$, find the set of values $(x,y)$ on which $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}$ exists."] [17592186124184 :question/worked-solution "Remember that the partial derivative of a function exists where the original function is defined *and* the partial derivative is defined.\n\nFirst, notice that $f(x,y)$ is not defined when $x=0$. So, the domain of $f$ is\n\n$$\nD_f = \\{(x,y) \\,: \\, x\\ne 0 \\}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivative of $f(x,y)$ with respect to $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_{y}(x, y) &= \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{y}{x}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe partial derivative $f_y(x,y)$ is also not defined for $x=0$. So, the domain of $f_y$ is\n\n$$\nD_{f_y} = \\{(x,y) \\,: \\, x\\ne 0 \\}\n$$\n\nTo compute the set where\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}\n$$\n\nexists, we take the intersection of the domain of the original function $(D_f)$ with the domain of the partial derivative $(D_{f_y})$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD_{f}∩D_{f_{y}} &= {(x, y): x \\ne 0}∩{(x, y): x \\ne 0} \\\\\n&= {(x, y): x \\ne 0}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}\n$$\n\nexists on the set\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\,: \\, x \\neq 0 \\}\n$$"] [17592186124284 :question/problem "Given that $f(x,y)= \\ln\\left(\\dfrac{x}{y^2}\\right)$ and\n$\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} = \\dfrac{1}{x}, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y} = -\\dfrac{2}{y}$,\nfind the set of values $(x,y)$ on which all partial derivatives of $f$ exist."] [17592186124284 :question/worked-solution "Remember that the partial derivatives of a function exist where the original function is defined and the partial derivatives are defined.\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = \\ln\\left(\\dfrac{x}{y^2}\\right)\n$$\n\nis defined only when\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x}{y^2}>0\n$$\n\nSo, the domain of $f$ is\n\n$$\nD_f = \\{(x,y) \\,: \\, x > 0, \\: y \\neq 0 \\}\n$$\n\nAs for the partial derivatives, we have the following:\n\n- The partial derivative $f_x(x,y) = \\dfrac{1}{x}$ is defined only when $x \\neq 0$. So, the domain of $f_x$ is\n$D_{f_x} = \\{(x,y) \\,: \\, x \\neq 0 \\}$.\n- The partial derivative $f_y(x,y) = -\\dfrac{2}{y}$ is defined only when $y \\neq 0$. So, the domain of $f_y$ is\n$D_{f_y} = \\{(x,y) \\,: \\, y \\neq 0 \\}$.\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partial derivatives of $f$ exist on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD_{f}∩D_{f_{x}}∩D_{f_{y}} &= {(x, y): x > 0, y \\ne 0}∩{(x, y): x \\ne 0}∩{(x, y): y \\ne 0} \\\\\n&= {(x, y): x > 0, y \\ne 0}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186124185 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $f(x,y,z)=xz\\ln{(x+y+z)}$?\n\n1. $f_x(-1,-1,-1)$ is defined\n2. $f_y(1,0,-1)$ is defined\n3. $f_z(1,1,1)$ is defined"] [17592186124185 :question/worked-solution "First, we compute the partial derivatives of $f(x,y,z)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x} &= z\\ln (x + y + z) + \\frac{xz}{x + y + z} \\\\\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y} &= \\frac{xz}{x + y + z} \\\\\n\\frac{∂f}{∂z} &= x\\ln (x + y + z) + \\frac{xz}{x + y + z}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine the statement one-by-one.\n\n- Statement I is false. If we substitute $(-1,-1,-1)$ into $f$, we get\n$f(-1,-1,-1) = (-1)(-1)\\ln{(-1-1-1)} = \\ln{(-3)}$,\nwhich is undefined since the logarithm exists only for positive arguments. Therefore, $f_x$ cannot exist at this point.\n- Statement II is false. If we substitute $(1,0,-1)$ into $f_y$, we get\n$f(1,0,-1) = \\dfrac{(1)(-1)}{1+0-1} = \\dfrac{-1}{0}$,\nwhich is undefined since we have division by zero. Since the function is not defined at the point, the derivative is also undefined.\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, the function $f$ is defined at $(1,1,1)$:\n$f(1,1,1)= (1)(1)\\ln(1+1+1)=\\ln(3)$\nLikewise $f_z$ is also defined at $(1,1,1)$:\n$f_{z}(1, 1, 1)|= (1)\\ln (1 + 1 + 1) + ((1)(1))/(1 + 1 + 1) = \\ln (3) + \\frac{1}{3}$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."]] "immutable_answers" [17592186122489 17592186122490 17592186122491 17592186122492 17592186122493 17592186122519 17592186122520 17592186122521 17592186122522 17592186122523] "immutable_fields" [[17592186122488 :answer-field/correct] [17592186122488 :answer-field/id] [17592186122488 :answer-field/key] [17592186122488 :answer-field/type] [17592186122518 :answer-field/correct] [17592186122518 :answer-field/id] [17592186122518 :answer-field/key] [17592186122518 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
